.th SLUP I "78/01/15"
.ds S SLUP
.sh NAME
slup - source librarian and update program
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd slup
library -[function][subswitch][/identifier]... file1 file2 ... filen
.sh DESCRIPTION
\*S is an archiver or librarian,
a program that maintains groups of files combined
into a single library file.
It is loosely base on the CDC Kronos Modify utility,
and has the ability to make logged modifications to
source programs stored in these libraries.
.s3
When a file is copied into the library,
it is called a module,
and takes the last argument of the file pathname
as the module name.
Any sets of modifications are called modsets and
must be given a modset name, unique within the module.
.s3
In use,
files are incorporated into the library file,
the modules are modified under the direction of directives
supplied through "standard input",
at which point they may be extracted.
Extraction simply creates a file separate from the
library with the same name as the module,
containing the module with any modifications included.
If any errors are discovered in update directives
or anything else before the library itself is actually
modified,
the library is preserved intact.
.s3
It is possible to "yank" and "unyank" a modset from the
library.
This means that the modset information is left inside
the library,
but,
flagged as inactive.
.s3
When \*S is called without arguments it will print out
a very brief summary of the available commands.
.s3
When given only a library name, \*S tests the validity of
the named file to ensure that it is indeed a source library.
.s3
If \*S is called with more than one argument then the following
list of switches applies.
Note that if no switch is supplied then the assumed switch
is "\fB-i\fR".
.s3
All switches except group function switches may be
followed by `\fBv\fR' for verbose.
This usually means that the name of the module gets typed
before the operation is attempted.
In the case of the "\fB-l\fR" switch, it actually gives
a much more informative output.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-n[v]
.br
Create a new library.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-i[v]
.br
Requires a following list of names of files which are to
be inserted into the library file.
It is an error if the library already contains
a module with the same name.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-r[v]
.br
Identical in operation to "\fB-i\fR" except that modules
already contained in the library may be replaced.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-l[a][g][m][x][d][v]
.br
List the names of all of the modules contained in the
library.
If a list of names is supplied, then only they are listed.
.lp +10 5
`\fBa\fR':	List all possible things.
.lp +10 5
`\fBg\fR':	List the defined group names for the library.
.lp +10 5
`\fBm\fR':	Lists all of the modset names used anywhere
within the library.
.lp +10 5
`\fBx\fR':	Give a cross-reference of which modules were
modified by which modsets.
.lp +10 5
`\fBd\fR':	List the documentation comments associated with
each modset.
.lp +10 5
`\fBv\fR':	List the insertion dates and the names of any
modifications alongside the module names.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-x[s][v]
.br
Extract the named modules from the library.
This means that the modules are copied from the library
file into new files
with the same names as the modules.
If no names are given,
then every module in the library is extracted.
.lp +10 5
`\fBs\fR':	Extract a version of the module with
handling any of the "\fB*call\fR" or "\fB*prefixc\fR"
extract-time directives.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-xo[v]
.br
Extract a version of the named modules that corresponds
to the original modules without any modifications
in effect.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-xu[v][/modsets]...
.br
Used to produce a file which when
supplied to \*S as update directives would make the
modifications necessary to reproduce the current library.
Unless the output filename is explicitly specified with
the "\fB-o\fR" switch,
it will be constructed by adding a suffix of ".mod" to
the library name.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-p[a][c][d][y][l][s][0][v]
.br
Similar to `\fB-x\fR' in that this function
extracts a module from the library.
The difference is that no source file is produced.
Instead, a listing is produced on the printer describing
the module in the library.
The sub-switches are used to select different printing options.
.lp +10 5
`\fB0\fR':	Produce just the basic listing without
any extra options.
.lp +10 5
`\fBa\fR':	Selects all printing options.
.lp +10 5
`\fBc\fR':	Allows included common modules to be listed.
.lp +10 5
`\fBd\fR':	Prints any deleted lines.
.lp +10 5
`\fBl\fR':	Allows directives to be printed.
.lp +10 5
`\fBs\fR':	ignore extraction-time directives.
.lp +10 5
`\fBy\fR':	Prints any lines contained in yanked modification sets.
i.e. "\fB*call\fR" and "\fB*prefixc\fR".
.lp +10 5
The default if no sub-switches are used is: "\fBd\fR".
.s3
.lp +5 5
-d[v]
.br
Delete the named modules from the library.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-du[v]/modset...
.br
Delete named modsets from each of the
named source modules in the library.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-u[p]
.lp +5 5
-t[p]
.br
Update the modules in the library.
The update directives are taken from standard input
(i.e. at the terminal).
For the format and description of the directives to update
the source modules see below.
If the `\fBp\fR' sub-switch is used then a report will be
produced describing the effect of the update run.
The `\fBt\fR' (test) function is different from
`\fBu\fR' (update) in the following respects:
the library itself is not altered in any way;
the temporary updated modules are always extracted
immediately after the update.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-c[+/-]
.br
Depending on the sign specified,
this switch will either give (`\fB+\fR') or take away (`\fB-\fR')
common status of a module,
which prevents the module being extracted unless
a "\fB-xa\fR" command line switch is used.
This is a convenience for modules that are only
"\fIcalled\fR".
.s3
.lp +5 5
-e[char]
.br
Specify the default escape or prefix character to be used
by the `\fB-x\fR' and `\fB-u\fR' switches.
This changes the initial escape character used to specify
directives,
and also,
the character used when the "\fB*prefix\fR" and the "\fB*prefixc\fR"
commands are used without any argument.
If no character is specified then the original default
character is used, i.e. `*'.
May be helpful if the file being extracted has a number
of asterisks in the first column.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-o filename
.br
Specify the filename to be used for list output from the
`\fB-p\fR' switch.
If no output file is specified, then the file is spooled
for printing on the line printer.
The same switch may be used to specify the
output filename used for "\fB-xu\fR".
.i0
.dt
.s3
For convenience,
\*S allows a "\fIgroupname\fR" to be given to a set of modules.
Modules may optionally be included in any of up to 16 named
groups.
If this is done,
it is possible to extract one or more groups
of modules by name.
This allows very simple grouping of modules,
still allowing some modules to be common to a number of packages.
The group functions are supposed to have group specifiers
instead of modset specifers in their command lines.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-g/groupname...
.br
The modules in the specified groups will be involved in
some other function to be specifed with another
function switch.
.br
e.g.
.br
slup mylib -g/group1/group2 -xv
.s3
.lp +5 5
-gm/groupname...
.br
Create the specified groups within the library file.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-gr/groupname...
.br
Remove these groupnames from the library.
No modules are affected.
.s3
.lp +5 5
-gi/groupname...
.br
Add the named modules to the specified groups.
.br
e.g.
.br
slup mylib -g/newgroup file1.c file2.s file3.h
.s3
.lp +5 5
-gd/groupname...
.br
Drop these modules from the specified groups.
.i0
.dt
.s3
UPDATE DIRECTIVES
.s3
When in update mode ("\fB-u\fR" or "\fB-t\fR" switches),
\*S accepts directives from standard input which are used
to specify any changes to be made to the source modules contained
in the current library.
.s3
The update directives are structured somewhat like editor
commands.
They all begin with an \fBescape\fR character which is by
default `*', but may be changed.
Any line which doesn't look like a directive is assumed
to be a text line to be inserted into the last referenced
source module.
If it is desired to enter a text line beginning with the
escape character, then the escape character may itself be
escaped by preceding it with a reverse-slant (`\\').
All modification sets applied to a source module
must be given a name.
This must be different from all modification set names
already associated with that particular source module.
The modification set name may have up to 14 characters.
.s3
To actually make any modifications,
it is necessary to give a name to the modset with
the "\fB*ident\fR" directive.
Then it is necessary to specify which module with
a "\fB*deck\fR" directive.
.s3
Some of the update directives require a line-specifier.
This is formatted thus: "[modsetname.]linenumber"
.br
The null modsetname refers to the original source module.
The format of the line-specifier will now be: ".[linenumber]".
If only a linenumber is given then a default name
is used.
This is set with the "\fB*modname\fR" directive and
is initially the null modsetname.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*p [char]
.lp +5 5
*prefix [char]
.br
Change the current escape character to "char".
If no character is supplied, the the default is used.
This is a `*' unless set by the `\fB-e\fR' switch.
The change remains in force until the end of the \*S
run unless another "\fB*prefix\fR" directive is
encountered in the meantime.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*dk filename
.lp +5 5
*deck filename
.br
Specify the name of the next module to be updated by \*S
in this update run.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*nd filename
.lp +5 5
*newdeck filename
.br
Set \*S up to create a new module within the library.
It is an error to attempt to recreate an existing module.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*id modset
.lp +5 5
*ident modset
.br
Name to be given to this modification set.
It must be unique for the module.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*m [modset]
.lp +5 5
*modname [modset]
.br
A name to be used as the default name in any following
line address specifiers.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*a linespec
.lp +5 5
*after linespec
.lp +5 5
*i linespec
.lp +5 5
*insert linespec
.br
Specify a position in the module to insert some text.
Any text following this directive is inserted after the
specified line.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*b linespec
.lp +5 5
*before linespec
.br
Has a similar function to "\fB*after\fR" but allows text
to be inserted before a line instead of after.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*ap
.lp +5 5
*append
.br
Similar to after/before but appends to the end of the module.
This is useful if it is required to add a subroutine to the
end of a module and the number of lines is not known.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*d linespec1[,linespec2]
.lp +5 5
*delete linespec1[,linespec2]
.br
Deletes all text between the two line-specifiers, inclusive.
"Linespec1" must be less than or equal to "linespec2" in value.
If only one line-specifier is supplied then only that line will
be deleted.
It should be pointed out that the deleted text is simply
flagged within the library without actually losing the
deleted lines.
This means that the deleted text may be recovered by
yanking the deleting modset,
or by including a "\fB*restor\fR" directive in a
later modset.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*r linespec1[,linespec2]
.lp +5 5
*restore linespec1[,linespec2]
.br
Restores all previously deleted text between the two
line-specifiers.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*y modsetname[,*]
.lp +5 5
*y modsetname1,modsetname2
.lp +5 5
*yank modsetname[,*]
.lp +5 5
*yank modsetname1,modsetname2
.br
Will yank the specified modification sets from this module.
The form containing the asterisk will yank in chronological
order all modsets since and including the named modset.
The form containing the two modset names will yank that
range.
It is an error if the two modsets were not created in
that order.
When a modification set is yanked then anything that that
modification set did will be ignored.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*u modsetname[,*]
.lp +5 5
*u modsetname1,modsetname2
.lp +5 5
*unyank modsetname[,*]
.lp +5 5
*unyank modsetname1,modsetname2
.br
Unyanks the specified modification set from this module.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*e
.lp +5 5
*exit
.br
Exit from this update run.
Mainly supplied so that \*S may be used in update mode within
command files.
.i0
.dt
.s3
Some directives are handled as normal text lines during
an update run.
These have special significance during an extraction run
however.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*c filename
.lp +5 5
*call filename
.br
When the module is extracted,
then the specified module in the
library is included at this point.
The included module will often be a "common" module.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*pc [char]
.lp +5 5
*prefixc [char]
.br
Change the escape character used when extracting the
modules to "char".
The default character is `*'.
The default of `*' may be overridden by the "-e" switch
on the command line.
The escape character set by this command remains in effect
until the end of the current module,
("call"ed modules don't count),
or until another "\fB*prefixc\fR" directive.
.i0
.dt
.s3
There are two directives simply used for documentation
purposes.
.s3
.lp +5 5
*/ ....
.lp +5 5
** ....
.br
Allows comments to be inserted with the update directives.
These are not included upon extraction of any modules.
The comments prefixed `**' are simply flushed from the input
stream when they are read.
The others however are saved within the library,
any may be listed by the "-ld" command line switch.
These comments also appear the file produced by
the "-xu" command.
.i0
.dt
.sh FILES
/tmp/slup#
.sh "SEE ALSO"
Modify Reference Manual (Control Data \(rg) (60281700F)
